[
    {
        "content": "<p>Looking at the following snippet in Lean core</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">false_imp_iff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iff_true_intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elim</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">imp_self</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iff_true_intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Is there a reason why <code>false_imp_iff</code> is not simp decorated but <code>imp_self</code> is? To me both eliminate from left to right just fine.</p>",
        "id": 599702875,
        "sender_full_name": "Plamen Dimitrov",
        "timestamp": 1780468406
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=false_implies#doc\">docs#false_implies</a> is being used in place of the first one</p>",
        "id": 599703966,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1780468858
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmmm, but <code>false_implies</code> is introduced by Mathlib and I was referring to the Lean core here. This either implies excessive coupling between the two projects or the simp can simply be enabled in Lean core where it makes more sense and reused entirely by Mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 599705609,
        "sender_full_name": "Plamen Dimitrov",
        "timestamp": 1780469469
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh, <code>false_implies</code> is from core. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs</a> also contains documentation for core; you can recognize core lean modules with the <code>Init.</code>/<code>Std.</code> and <code>Lean.</code> prefixes, in this case we have <code>Init.SimpLemmas</code>.</p>",
        "id": 599708582,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1780470398
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"873547\">Robin Arnez</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Non-normalizable.20core.20.60false_imp_iff.60.20theorem/near/599708582\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Oh, <code>false_implies</code> is from core. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs</a> also contains documentation for core; you can recognize core lean modules with the <code>Init.</code>/<code>Std.</code> and <code>Lean.</code> prefixes, in this case we have <code>Init.SimpLemmas</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ok, it is still a bit strange that one project contains the documentation of another project but let's run with this (and thank for the pointer for looking at the namespaces and prefixes!). In this case I would rephrase my question:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">false_imp_iff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iff_true_intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elim</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">imp_self</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iff_true_intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Why isn't there <code>self_implies</code> in Mathlib too with <code>imp_self</code> not using <code>@[simp high]</code>? This is somewhat adjoint to the original question - why is <code>imp_self</code> using simp but <code>false_imp_iff</code> not?</p>",
